WEBVTT

1
00:00:16.178 --> 00:00:20.361
said you needed a cushion," Laurie said, walking into Grandpa's study.

2
00:00:20.381 --> 00:00:22.942
"I brought two just in case."

3
00:00:22.962 --> 00:00:25.905
"Yes, I do need a cushion.

4
00:00:26.045 --> 00:00:27.946
Thank you," Grandpa said.

5
00:00:28.446 --> 00:00:30.528
He was sitting cross-legged on the floor.

6
00:00:32.124 --> 00:00:36.267
"I'm giving up chairs and devoting myself to deep thinking for one week.

7
00:00:37.448 --> 00:00:42.211
Maybe if I sit like Buddha and try to think like Buddha, I'll be able to write a

8
00:00:42.291 --> 00:00:45.113
great article on Buddha's life in ancient India."

9
00:00:46.474 --> 00:00:49.276
He picked up a small statue of the famous monk.

10
00:00:49.296 --> 00:00:52.779
"Wait—Buddha was a real person?"

11
00:00:53.219 --> 00:00:55.641
Laurie asked, sitting down next to Grandpa.

12
00:00:59.988 --> 00:01:03.589
He was a real prince, named Siddhartha Gautama.

13
00:01:04.629 --> 00:01:10.411
He was born around the sixth century BC to the king and queen of the Shakya kingdom.

14
00:01:11.831 --> 00:01:18.473
Back then, the areas we know as India and Nepal were made up of small, independent kingdoms.

15
00:01:19.533 --> 00:01:27.676
Hinduism, which had been practiced in India for hundreds of years, divided society into four rigid classes, or

16
00:01:27.756 --> 00:01:34.127
castes. There were priests, royalty, merchants and farmers, and laborers.

17
00:01:35.948 --> 00:01:40.710
Since Siddhartha was born into a royal family, he enjoyed a life of luxury.

18
00:01:42.271 --> 00:01:48.015
His father wanted to protect his son from all the unpleasant things in the world, so he forbade

19
00:01:48.075 --> 00:01:49.816
Siddhartha from leaving the palace.

20
00:01:50.896 --> 00:01:54.018
But you know what happens when you tell people they can't do something.

21
00:01:54.978 --> 00:01:57.380
That becomes exactly what they want to do!

22
00:01:58.568 --> 00:02:01.670
So one day Siddhartha sneaked out of the palace.

23
00:02:02.170 --> 00:02:07.333
And he was shocked by what he saw: old people, sick people, and dead people!

24
00:02:09.914 --> 00:02:14.257
He saw great suffering and wondered why there was so much pain in the world.

25
00:02:15.557 --> 00:02:20.160
Then he met a hermit who said he had given up wealth to search for truth.

26
00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:23.682
Siddhartha decided he would do that too.

27
00:02:24.202 --> 00:02:27.584
So he left his kingdom and set out to lead a hermit's life.

28
00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:30.321
Siddhartha thought.

29
00:02:30.802 --> 00:02:31.662
And he fasted.

30
00:02:32.083 --> 00:02:32.843
And he walked.

31
00:02:33.884 --> 00:02:38.428
Then finally he sat beneath a tree by a river and thought very, very hard.

32
00:02:40.010 --> 00:02:45.034
After some time, he realized the truth about the world and why people suffer.

33
00:02:47.516 --> 00:02:52.261
It was under that tree that he became a buddha, or an 'enlightened one.'

34
00:02:53.402 --> 00:02:55.904
That means he truly understood the world."

35
00:02:56.992 --> 00:03:00.655
Grandpa closed his eyes and put his hands back over his knees.

36
00:03:00.675 --> 00:03:02.717
"Good story, eh?"

37
00:03:03.097 --> 00:03:03.558
he said.

38
00:03:04.398 --> 00:03:05.900
He sat very still.

39
00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:09.062
"Yes, a very good story.

40
00:03:10.023 --> 00:03:12.685
But aren't you going to tell me what he found out?

41
00:03:13.466 --> 00:03:14.887
And what happened after that?

42
00:03:15.868 --> 00:03:18.350
Did he get up or sit there forever?"

43
00:03:18.851 --> 00:03:20.332
Laurie asked impatiently.

44
00:03:21.273 --> 00:03:22.554
Grandpa looked at Laurie.

45
00:03:22.574 --> 00:03:24.996
"He didn't stay there forever.

46
00:03:25.572 --> 00:03:31.635
After he became enlightened, he set out to teach people what he had discovered, because he thought it

47
00:03:31.655 --> 00:03:32.315
would help them.

48
00:03:33.535 --> 00:03:37.517
He had discovered that people suffer because they have desires.

49
00:03:38.657 --> 00:03:42.559
They want things, and when they don't get them, they become upset or sad.

50
00:03:43.639 --> 00:03:47.701
In order to avoid suffering, people must get rid of their desires.

51
00:03:48.821 --> 00:03:50.102
This is what Buddha taught.

52
00:03:51.202 --> 00:03:54.944
He encouraged people to lead modest, moderate lives.

53
00:03:56.064 --> 00:04:02.606
He also encouraged them to ignore the Hindu caste system, because he believed that all people were equal.

54
00:04:04.286 --> 00:04:08.367
Hindus believed that only men in high castes could study religion.

55
00:04:09.408 --> 00:04:12.689
But Buddha said everyone was free to learn his teachings.

56
00:04:13.789 --> 00:04:21.171
Buddha had become enlightened in the powerful Magadha kingdom, which ruled northern India, and his story was famous

57
00:04:21.231 --> 00:04:26.997
there. The Magadha king, Bimbisara, invited Buddha to his palace.

58
00:04:28.458 --> 00:04:34.782
There Buddha taught King Bimbisara his knowledge of the world, and the king was so moved that he

59
00:04:34.843 --> 00:04:35.723
became a Buddhist.

60
00:04:36.884 --> 00:04:39.446
He offered one of his gardens to the wise Buddha.

61
00:04:40.506 --> 00:04:44.329
Siddhartha used the garden as a temporary home and teaching place.

62
00:04:45.550 --> 00:04:50.453
Many people learned about Buddhism there, and the popularity of his religion increased.

63
00:04:51.574 --> 00:04:54.756
He continued to teach until he died at age 80.

64
00:04:55.444 --> 00:04:58.986
By that time Buddhism was firmly established in India."

65
00:04:59.006 --> 00:05:03.068
"What happened to Buddhism after he died?"

66
00:05:03.509 --> 00:05:04.269
Laurie asked.

67
00:05:05.510 --> 00:05:13.194
"Even after his death, Buddhism continued to spread throughout India, especially thanks to King Ashoka of the great

68
00:05:13.334 --> 00:05:14.375
Mauryan kingdom.

69
00:05:15.415 --> 00:05:17.616
He was a vicious warrior and conqueror.

70
00:05:18.597 --> 00:05:27.603
During his rule, from about 270 BC to 232 BC, he attacked neighboring kingdoms and led wars to

71
00:05:27.663 --> 00:05:30.766
expand his territory, unifying much of India.

72
00:05:32.107 --> 00:05:38.773
After one particularly bloody war, however, he suddenly found himself horrified at his actions.

73
00:05:39.894 --> 00:05:43.017
He asked himself whether a violent life was worth living.

74
00:05:44.078 --> 00:05:50.764
Then he remembered the teachings of Buddha, which encouraged peace, equality, and harmony among all living things.

75
00:05:52.048 --> 00:05:55.230
Ashoka decided this was how people should live.

76
00:05:56.371 --> 00:06:00.394
He converted to Buddhism and declared his land a Buddhist kingdom.

77
00:06:01.755 --> 00:06:05.617
He wanted everyone to learn Buddha's teachings and live in harmony.

78
00:06:06.758 --> 00:06:12.382
Ashoka helped spread Buddhism throughout India, and for a while his kingdom flourished.

79
00:06:13.403 --> 00:06:19.287
But as you may guess, being peaceful only works if you can convince your neighbors and the next

80
00:06:19.387 --> 00:06:20.568
king to be peaceful too.

81
00:06:21.784 --> 00:06:28.627
After Ashoka's death political conflict and foreign invaders led to the end of the Mauryan kingdom and the

82
00:06:28.787 --> 00:06:30.848
end of a unified, peaceful India.

83
00:06:32.288 --> 00:06:38.731
In the years to come, Christians and Muslims would come from the west, forever changing Indian culture.

84
00:06:40.252 --> 00:06:46.494
Buddhism lost its power in India, but traveled north and east to take root in China, Korea, and

85
00:06:46.534 --> 00:06:49.235
Japan." Grandpa breathed deeply.

86
00:06:52.968 --> 00:06:58.313
"Now let's think about life, suffering, desire, peace, and enlightenment."

87
00:06:58.853 --> 00:07:00.635
He pointed to the extra cushion.

88
00:07:05.579 --> 00:07:11.784
this?" Laurie sat cross-legged, placed her hands over her knees, and closed her eyes.

89
00:07:11.804 --> 00:07:20.672
"Exactly. That is how Siddhartha sat, and so shall we," Grandpa said, closing his eyes.

90
00:07:21.864 --> 00:07:24.266
Laurie opened one eye to peek at Grandpa.

91
00:07:24.786 --> 00:07:26.648
He appeared to be deep in thought.

92
00:07:26.668 --> 00:07:33.173
"Grandpa, it's pretty cool that you know all this stuff," she whispered.

93
00:07:34.194 --> 00:07:36.496
Then she closed her eyes and thought.
